// TASK 1: Introduction
$task_contents[1] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>Cross-Site Scripting, commonly called XSS, is one of the most persistent vulnerabilities in web application security. It happens when an application takes untrusted input and places it into a page without properly checking or encoding it, allowing an attacker's script to run inside another user's browser. Despite being decades old, XSS still appears in modern applications because the underlying mistake, trusting input that should never be trusted, is easy to make and easy to overlook.</p>

    <p>By the end of this room, you will be able to identify the three major types of XSS, explain how each one is exploited, and recognize the difference between an application that handles input safely and one that does not. A hands-on lab is included where you will inject and trigger your own payloads inside a simulated comment system to see the vulnerability in action.</p>

    <h3>Learning Objectives</h3>
