// TASK 2: What is Cross-Site Scripting?
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>When Input Becomes Code</h2>

    <p>Every web application takes input from users somewhere, a search box, a comment field, a URL parameter. That input is supposed to remain data, text to display, not instructions to run. Cross-Site Scripting happens when an application blurs that line and lets user input execute as code inside someone else's browser.</p>

    <p>Think of a restaurant waiter who reads customer orders aloud to the kitchen exactly as they are written. If a customer writes "one steak" the waiter reads "one steak". But if a customer manages to write a note that also tells the kitchen to give free dessert to every table, and the waiter reads it aloud without questioning it, the kitchen treats it as a real instruction. XSS works the same way. The browser is the waiter, and unsanitized input is the sneaky note.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>XSS is consistently ranked among the most common vulnerabilities found in real production applications. It does not require breaking encryption or bypassing a firewall, only finding one place where input is trusted a little too much.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>The Trust Boundary</h3>
    <p>Browsers enforce a rule called the <strong>same-origin policy</strong>, which normally stops one website from reading or interfering with another. XSS does not break this rule directly. Instead, it tricks the vulnerable site into running the attacker's script as if the site itself had written it, so the script inherits all the trust the browser already has in that site.</p>

