// TASK 3: Reflected XSS
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>The One-Time Echo</h2>

    <p>Reflected XSS is the simplest and most common form of XSS. The malicious payload is not stored anywhere. It travels inside a single request, usually a URL parameter, and the server immediately reflects it back into the page's response.</p>

    <p>Picture someone handing you a note and asking you to read it aloud right away. You read it, the moment passes, and the note is gone. Nothing is saved for the next person. Reflected XSS behaves the same way, it only affects the one victim who was tricked into sending that specific request.</p>

    <p>Because the payload never gets saved, the attacker must actively deliver it to the victim, usually through a phishing link containing the malicious script as a URL parameter. If the victim never clicks the link, the attack never happens.</p>

