// TASK 4: Stored XSS
$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>The Payload That Stays</h2>

    <p>Stored XSS, also called persistent XSS, takes the same idea further. Instead of relying on a crafted link every time, the attacker submits the malicious payload once, and the application saves it, typically inside a database. From that point on, the payload runs automatically for every visitor who views the affected page.</p>

    <p>This is closer to spray painting graffiti on a public wall instead of handing someone a single note. Nobody needs to be tricked into reading it. Anyone who simply walks past the wall sees it, over and over, until it is removed.</p>

    <p>A classic example is a comment field on a blog post. If the application saves a comment containing a script without checking it, every future visitor who loads that page runs the attacker's script the moment the page renders, with no phishing link required.</p>

