// TASK 5: DOM-based XSS and Impact
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>When the Browser Never Talks to the Server</h2>

    <p>DOM-based XSS is different from the first two because the server is never involved in the attack at all. A client-side script reads untrusted data, often something after a hash symbol in the URL, and inserts it directly into the page. Because the browser processes this entirely on its own, the malicious code never has to pass through the server to be logged or filtered.</p>

    <p>This is like passing a note entirely within your own house, with no mail carrier involved at any point. The vulnerability lives inside the page's own JavaScript, not in how the server builds the response.</p>

    <h3>What Attackers Actually Do With It</h3>
    <p>Regardless of which of the three types is used, the goal is usually the same: steal something valuable from the victim's browser or send them somewhere dangerous. The two most common outcomes are session cookie theft and malicious redirects.</p>

    <p>A session cookie is what keeps a user logged in without retyping a password on every page. If an attacker's script can read that cookie and send it to a server they control, they can impersonate the victim without ever knowing their password.</p>

    <div class="warning-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Session Hijacking</h4>
        <p>An attacker holding a stolen session cookie can act as the victim inside the application immediately, with no login required. This is why cookies are frequently marked HttpOnly, a setting that blocks JavaScript from reading them at all.</p>
    </div>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Origin of Payload</th>
                <th>Server Involvement</th>
                <th>Persistence</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Reflected XSS</td>
                <td>URL parameter in a crafted link</td>
                <td>Server reflects input back</td>
                <td>Does not persist</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Stored XSS</td>
                <td>Saved user input, such as a comment</td>
                <td>Server stores and serves it</td>
                <td>Persists until removed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>DOM-based XSS</td>
                <td>Client-side script reads untrusted data</td>
                <td>None, entirely in the browser</td>
                <td>Does not persist</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>In the practical that follows, you will see this exact cookie theft scenario play out inside a simulated comment system, from the first reflected payload all the way to a stolen session value.</p>
</div>
HTML;

