$task_contents[7] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <p>Cross-Site Scripting is a reminder that the browser will run whatever code it is given, without asking who wrote it. Reflected XSS lives for a single request, stored XSS persists for every future visitor, and DOM-based XSS never even touches the server. All three share the same root cause: untrusted input treated as trusted code.</p>

    <p>Defending against XSS comes down to consistently encoding output and never assuming user input is safe, no matter how small or temporary it looks.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>XSS happens when user input is executed as code instead of displayed as text.</li>
        <li>Reflected XSS exists for a single request and requires a crafted link.</li>
        <li>Stored XSS persists on the server and affects every future visitor.</li>
        <li>DOM-based XSS runs entirely client-side with no server involvement.</li>
        <li>Stolen session cookies let an attacker impersonate a victim without a password.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
